   "id": "e26ece9c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "client = OpenAI()\n",
    "\n",
    "response = client.chat.completions.create(\n",
    "    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n",
    "    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain quantum computing to a duck\"}],\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "97355b7f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "'Alright, little duck! Imagine you have a very special pond where you can swim in two different ways at the same time—one way is fast, and the other is slow. In the world of quantum computing, we use tiny things called qubits that can also be in two states at once, like being both 0 and 1 at the same time. This is a bit like how you can waddle on land and swim in water simultaneously!\\n\\nWhen qubits work together in this special pond of quantum computing, they can solve complicated problems much faster than regular computers—like how you could find the biggest and best food in the pond super quickly! So, quantum computing helps us perform many calculations all at once, just like you could bob around the pond in many directions at the same time, looking for tasty snacks. Quack! 🦆'"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 3,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
